WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.620
How do you test what steers a growing nerve fibre?

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:08.440
That fibre is an axon, led by a flat, moving fan called the growth cone.

3
00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.140
Molecules outside, called guidance cues, pull or push it.

4
00:00:15.933 --> 00:00:20.753
The growth cone probes its surface with actin spikes, sheets, and cue-sensing

5
00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:26.433
receptors. When a cue hits one side more strongly, it turns toward or away.

6
00:00:27.913 --> 00:00:32.213
Cues act on contact or across distance, so each needs its own dish test.

7
00:00:33.317 --> 00:00:38.957
To test cues across distance, embed living tissue pieces, called explants, in a

8
00:00:38.957 --> 00:00:44.917
collagen gel. A secreted cue spreads through the gel, drawing axons toward the

9
00:00:44.917 --> 00:00:46.257
source or pushing them away.

10
00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:52.817
To test surface cues, lay alternating narrow membrane lanes like a striped carpet.

11
00:00:54.077 --> 00:00:59.297
Place axons across the lanes so each growth cone meets border after border and

12
00:00:59.297 --> 00:01:00.297
chooses a lane.

13
00:01:01.217 --> 00:01:06.557
Flood a dish with repellent membranes, and responsive fans can collapse into thin

14
00:01:06.557 --> 00:01:07.757
tips within minutes.

15
00:01:08.857 --> 00:01:12.617
Release the cue from one pipette side, and the cone turns away.

16
00:01:13.997 --> 00:01:19.257
Every assay places cues around growth cones so motion separates attraction from

17
00:01:19.257 --> 00:01:20.257
repulsion.

18
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.780
Yet avoiding a lane can mislead.

19
00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:28.480
Migrating neural crest cells carry Eph receptors, and avoid stripes of their

20
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.180
partner, ephrin-B1.

21
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.360
A sceptic says that stripe is simply a poor surface.

22
00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:39.680
Now add soluble ephrin-B1, leaving the stripe surface unchanged.

23
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.760
If avoidance is active repulsion, what happens?

24
00:01:49.383 --> 00:01:55.043
The cells cross the stripes and spread evenly, as the 1997 chick study showed.

25
00:01:56.283 --> 00:02:01.063
Free ephrin fills Eph receptors everywhere, so the border no longer reads as

26
00:02:01.063 --> 00:02:06.523
different. A merely poor surface would still be avoided, because the stripe itself

27
00:02:06.523 --> 00:02:12.623
is unchanged. A protein that does not bind Eph receptors should leave avoidance

28
00:02:12.623 --> 00:02:13.623
intact.

29
00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:19.633
Layout, dose, and exposure time also shape whether a cone turns or collapses.

30
00:02:21.193 --> 00:02:23.733
Receptors decide whether a cone answers at all.

31
00:02:25.073 --> 00:02:29.333
Internal state, such as cyclic GMP level, can decide which way.

32
00:02:30.553 --> 00:02:34.253
And a gel shows steering across distance, not the molecule itself.

33
00:02:35.433 --> 00:02:41.853
In 1988, Marc Tessier-Lavigne and colleagues, in Thomas Jessell's laboratory, set

34
00:02:41.853 --> 00:02:45.273
embryonic rat spinal cord explants in collagen gel.

35
00:02:46.573 --> 00:02:51.733
Something from the floor plate crossed the gel, drawing midline-crossing axons; it

36
00:02:51.733 --> 00:02:52.933
was later named netrin.

37
00:02:54.253 --> 00:03:00.833
In 1990, Jonathan Raper and Josef Kapfhammer added embryonic brain membranes to

38
00:03:00.833 --> 00:03:02.813
chick dorsal root ganglion neurons.

39
00:03:03.973 --> 00:03:08.173
Cones collapsed within minutes, recovering once the membranes were gone.

40
00:03:09.267 --> 00:03:16.067
In 1987, Jochen Walter, Friedrich Bonhoeffer and colleagues striped anterior and

41
00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:19.327
posterior chick tectum membranes for retinal axons.

42
00:03:20.647 --> 00:03:26.967
In 1995, Drescher, Bonhoeffer and colleagues used that assay to purify a posterior

43
00:03:26.967 --> 00:03:30.787
tectum repellent. It is now called ephrin-A5.

44
00:03:32.087 --> 00:03:36.747
Across gels, stripes, and baths, growth cones show what attracts or repels.
